          %h2#idea Idea
          %p …
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p …
          %h2#universal_commutativity Universal commutativity
          %p
            In a locally
            = succeed "-presentable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "-filtered" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          %p
            In particular, in a locally finitely presentable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %p In an algebraic (∞,1)-category, i.e., one that is equivalent to the category of finite product-preserving functors from a small (∞,1)-category with finite products to the (∞,1)-category of spaces, sifted (∞,1)-colimits commute with finite (∞,1)-products.
          %h2#special_commutativity_model_categories Special commutativity: model ∞-categories
          %p
            From a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/287091/why-do-we-need-model-categories/287234#287234"} MathOverflow answer
          %p Model categories provide a powerful framework for commuting (homotopy) limits and colimits, and, more generally, for commuting left adjoint functors and (homotopy) limits, as well as right adjoint functors and (homotopy) colimits. (In what follows, I omit the adjective “homotopy” before limits and colimits.)
          %p In finitely presentable ∞-categories filtered colimits commute with finite limits and in finitely presentable ∞-categories with a set of compact projective generators sifted colimits commute with finite products. However, many other situations of interest are not covered by such statements. For instance, one might want to commute a sifted colimit past an infinite product, a pullback, or a cosifted limit (e.g., a cosimplicial totalization). One might also want to commute sifted colimits past finite products in ∞-categories that do not have a set of compact projective generators, e.g., the ∞-category of small ∞-categories.
          %p In all such situations the relevant statement is false at least for some diagrams, so whatever criterion we devise must analyze the specific diagrams at question. This is precisely what model categories achieve.
          %p
            For example, one might want to commute
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C\to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi lim
                    %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim_L: Fun(L,B)\to B,
          %p
            the limit functor for
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          %p
            We would like to devise a condition on a diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D: K\to C
            that would guarantee that the canonical comparison map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi K
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi K
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_K F(D) \to  F(colim_K D)
          %p
            is an equivalence in the ∞-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun(K,C)
            whose weak equivalences are created by the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_K
            (i.e., a natural transformation of functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K\to C
            is a weak equivalence if its
            = succeed "-colimit" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun(K,B)
            is turned into a relative ∞-category in the same way. The functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun(K,F): Fun(K,C)\to Fun(K,B)
          %p is a functor between ∞-categories that need not preserve weak equivalences.
          %p
            Now comes the (potentially) creative part: equip the relative ∞-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun(K,C)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun(K,B)
            with model structures such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fun(K,F)
            is a right Quillen functor. In many situations of interest this can be done immediately using existing tools.
          %p
            Our criterion now says that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D: K\to C
            is a
            %em fibrant
            diagram, then the comparison map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi K
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi K
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_K F(D) \to  F(colim_K D)
          %p
            is an equivalence in the ∞-category B, i.e., the colimit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            commutes with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            For instance, one can take
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K=\Delta^{op}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi spaces
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=spaces
            (alias ∞-groupoids),
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=Fun(L,B)
            (i.e.,
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi lim
                    %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F=lim_L: Fun(L,B)\to B,
          %p
            the
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            can be taken to be the pullback diagram or the infinite discrete category and the model structure can be taken to be the projective model structure. In this case we obtain a criterion for commuting
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^{op}
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          %p
            There are many variations on the above theme, for instance, one can consider
            %em weighted
            limits (in the sense of enriched category theory) and then derive the limit functor with respect to both the functor and the weight, which yields even more powerful computational tools etc.
          %p
            Many classical results on model categories fit in the above framework. For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a simplicial model category,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a cofibrant object, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a fibrant object, then the simplicial mapping space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(X,Y)
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            computes the mapping space in the ∞-localization of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with respect to its weak equivalences. We can fit this in the above framework by taking
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Fun
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Spaces
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F=Map(-,Y): Fun(\Delta^op,M)\to Fun(\Delta^op,Spaces),
          %p
            and observing that a cofibrant object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has a cofibrant cosimplicial resolution
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\otimes\Delta^n
          %p
            Textbook accounts include Chapter 7 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Cisinski"} Cisinski
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutativity+of+limits+and+colimits"} commutativity of limits and colimits
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%80-Kan+condition"} π-Kan condition
          %h2#references References
          %h3#universal_commutativity_2 Universal commutativity
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
              (2009)
          %h3#special_commutativity Special commutativity
          %p
            On conditions that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            commutes with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces"} realization
            (cf.
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%80-Kan+condition"} π-Kan condition
            and
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces#SufficientConditionsForRealizationToPreserveHomotopyPullback"} this Prop.
          %ul
            %li#BousfieldFriedlander78
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aldridge+Bousfield"}> Aldridge Bousfield
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Friedlander"} Eric Friedlander
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Homotopy theory of
                    = succeed "-spaces," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Γ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                %strong 658
                Springer (1978) 80-130 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://web.math.rochester.edu/people/faculty/doug/otherpapers/bousfield-friedlander.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BousfieldFriedlanderSpectra.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Lurie11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Simplicial spaces
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/287x.html"} Algebraic L-theory and Surgery
                (2011) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/287xnotes/Lecture7.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Rezk14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"}> Charles Rezk
                ,
                %em When are homotopy colimits compatible with homotopy base change?
                (2014) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://faculty.math.illinois.edu/~rezk/i-hate-the-pi-star-kan-condition.pdf"}> i-hate-the-pi-star-kan-condition.pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/RezkHomotopyColimitsBaseChange.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                , §A.5, esp. Def. A.5.2.1, Thms. A.5.3.1, A.5.4.1, A.5.6.1 in
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spectral+Algebraic+Geometry"} Spectral Algebraic Geometry
                (2018) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/SAG-rootfile.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p#ForATreatment
            For a treatment without ∞-categories, see also
            %a{:href => "#BEBP19"} BEBP19, Def. 3.2, Ex. 3.10, Thm. 3.17
            below.
          %p
            An even larger class of maps of so-called
            %em weak Kan fibrations
            is studied in Section 3 of
          %ul
            %li#BEBP19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Berwick-Evans"}> Daniel Berwick-Evans
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pedro+Boavida"} Pedro Boavida
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"} Dmitri Pavlov
              = succeed "," do
                %em Classifying spaces of infinity-sheaves
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10544"} arXiv:1912.10544
          %p
            The general theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} model (∞,1)-categories
            that allows one to commute homotopy colimits past homotopy limits is developed in a series of papers by Aaaron Mazel-Gee:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Mazel-Gee"}> Aaron Mazel-Gee
                ,
                %em Model ∞-categories I: some pleasant properties of the ∞-category of simplicial spaces
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.8411"}> arXiv:1412.8411
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Mazel-Gee"}> Aaron Mazel-Gee
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Model ∞-categories II: Quillen adjunctions
                %strong 27
                (2021) 508-550. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.04392"}> arXiv:1510.04392
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://nyjm.albany.edu/j/2021/27-21.html"} nyjm:27-21
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Mazel-Gee"}> Aaron Mazel-Gee
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Model ∞-categories III: the fundamental theorem
                %strong 27
                (2021) 551-599 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.04777"}> arXiv:1510.04777
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://nyjm.albany.edu/j/2021/27-22.html"} nyjm:27-22
          %p and in
          %ul
            %li#Cisinski
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              , Chapter 7 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Categories+and+Homotopical+Algebra"} Higher Categories and Homotopical Algebra
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 180
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108588737"} doi:10.1017/9781108588737
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://cisinski.app.uni-regensburg.de/CatLR.pdf"} pdf
          %p In particular, see Propositions 7.5.5, 7.7.4, 7.7.5.
